<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · c. 1824 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Tannenbaum</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German song from 1824 about the fir tree, which stays green all winter. It became a favourite Christmas song.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ernst Anschütz; first verse by August Zarnack</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (the melody of the student song &quot;Lauriger Horatius&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Leipzig, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1824, on a folk song known from the 16th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ernst Anschütz, published 1824, on a first verse by August Zarnack (1820))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-tannenbaum">O Tannenbaum</a>&quot; (&quot;O fir tree&quot;), known in English as &quot;O <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Tree&quot;, is a German Christmas song built on an older folk song that had nothing to do with the holiday. It draws on a long tradition in which the fir tree, green in every season, stood for faithfulness. As early as the 16th century songbooks held a text that gave rise to a folk song, &quot;O Tannenbaum, du trägst ein' grünen Zweig&quot; (&quot;O fir tree, you wear a green branch&quot;). The folk song collector Ludwig Erk found three different melodies for it in different regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which he set out in the 1856 edition of his Deutscher Liederhort; the tunes were written down only in the 18th and 19th centuries, but Melchior Franck had already quoted a line of the song on one of them in 1605, in his quodlibet &quot;Nun fanget an&quot;.</p><p>In 1820 the German educator August Zarnack printed &quot;Der Tannenbaum&quot; as No. 51 in the second volume of his collection of folk songs for schools. The old folk song may have inspired it, but Zarnack's text was a lover's complaint, setting the faithfulness of the fir tree against the fickleness of a young woman, and, as folklorists of German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> sometimes did, he may have written much or all of it himself in what he took to be a folk idiom. He set it to a melody published earlier with the song &quot;Es lebe hoch der Zimmermannsgeselle&quot;.</p><p>The words known today are by Ernst Anschütz, a teacher and organist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, who published them in his Musikalisches Schulgesangbuch in 1824. He kept Zarnack's first verse and wrote two more, linking the tree's faithful green to the joy and hope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, so that in German the song names Christmas only in its second verse. Anschütz printed it with the same &quot;Zimmermannsgeselle&quot; tune, but in time it came to be sung to the melody of &quot;Lauriger Horatius&quot;, an old student song. As the custom of decorating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-tree">Christmas trees</a> spread in these years, it became one of the best-known Christmas songs in both German and English, although it is not, strictly, about such a tree. Some versions sung today change &quot;treu&quot; (&quot;faithful&quot;) to &quot;grün&quot; (&quot;green&quot;) and the Christmas line to one about winter, and with the third verse, with its lessons drawn from religion, left out, the song can be sung as a secular one.</p><p>The tune has carried many other texts. It is the tune of &quot;The Red Flag&quot;, the anthem of the British and Irish Labour Parties, of the former state songs &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, My Maryland&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, My Florida&quot;, of &quot;Michigan, My Michigan&quot; and &quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Iowa&quot;, of the &quot;Ode to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a>&quot;, of college songs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, of the Boy Scouts' &quot;Scout Vespers&quot;, of a Swedish schoolchildren's song about the bus driver, and of &quot;Democrats, Good Democrats&quot;, a campaign song for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grover-cleveland">Grover Cleveland</a> in 1884.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The song speaks to the fir tree and praises its faithful green, set in Zarnack's first verse against human fickleness. In Anschütz's added verses that green becomes a sign of the joy and hope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, and the third verse draws a lesson of faith from it. The mood is warm and simple, a song for home and school.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wie treu sind deine Blätter;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How are thy leaves so verdant;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">du grünst nicht nur zur Sommerzeit,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Not only in the summer time</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">nein, auch im Winter, wenn es schneit.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But e'en in winter is thy prime;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wie treu sind deine Blätter.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>How are thy leaves so verdant?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">du kannst mir sehr gefallen;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We sing in happy measure.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wie oft hat nicht zur Weihnachtszeit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thy praise who dost our Christmas greet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">ein Baum von dir mich hoch erfreut.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With verdure fair and mem'ries sweet.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">du kannst mir sehr gefallen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tree of unfailing treasure.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">dein Kleid will mir was lehren:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thy garb unfading showeth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">die Hoffnung und Beständigkeit</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The flower of joy about my door,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">giebt Trost und Kraft zu jeder Zeit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good cheer that faileth nevermore.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Tannenbaum, o Tannenbaum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O evergreen, O evergreen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">dein Kleid will mir was lehren.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My heart thy lesson knoweth.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ernst Anschütz, Musikalisches Schulgesangbuch (1824), the first printing as quoted on German Wikipedia. Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Tannenbaum" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: &quot;German Folk Song&quot;, The Bay View Magazine 21 (4) (1914). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Bay_View_Magazine%2FVolume_21%2FNumber_4%2FGerman_Folk_Song" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
